{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H3jwaR3yyJZhcVnDccw3Dm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QzYEXLv7efWdwrRSLLeFnp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513_2/projects/recursive_gen_depth_est/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T140607/worst/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24261,"cache_read_input_tokens":56951,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":24261,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fr9E31omErFZDmot9fqjaq","session_id":"bc99c231-f3c4-48af-92ec-23a6c87c30e4","uuid":"c545012a-8230-497f-bdc0-7ad9926e6a1b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QzYEXLv7efWdwrRSLLeFnp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n6\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n11\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Measuring the Limits of Recursive Generation Depth Estimation:\\\\\n15\tA Depth Degradation Atlas Across Models and Decoding Strategies}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous \\\\\n18\t\\textit{EMNLP 2026 Submission}}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t% ============================================================\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tWeb-scraped corpora increasingly contain multi-generational synthetic text produced by uncontrolled model$\\to$data$\\to$model feedback loops.\n26\tExisting detectors classify text as real or synthetic but discard information about \\emph{how many recursive cycles} the text has undergone---a distinction that matters because first-generation synthetic data can be beneficial while deeply recursive text drives distribution collapse.\n27\tWe formalize \\textbf{ordinal generation depth estimation} as a new task, construct a benchmark spanning three model families (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) $\\times$ three decoding strategies (nucleus, top-$k$, temperature) $\\times$ six depths (0--5), and systematically characterize the fundamental limits of depth estimability.\n28\tOur primary contributions are: (1)~the \\textbf{Depth Degradation Atlas}, a comprehensive empirical map of how statistical features evolve across recursive depths, and (2)~the \\textbf{Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD)} metric, which we find equals~2 in 7 of 9 model--strategy combinations---meaning that beyond the second generation, adjacent depths become statistically indistinguishable.\n29\tWe evaluate multiple estimation approaches including a contrastive ordinal learning method (DCOL), finding that no method significantly outperforms simple ordinal baselines (DCOL 42\\% vs.\\ CORAL 40\\% 6-class accuracy, $p=0.12$), confirming the bottleneck lies in signal availability rather than learning methodology.\n30\tCross-model transfer accuracy drops to 33\\%, revealing that depth signals are model-specific and that practical deployment requires model fingerprinting as a prerequisite.\n31\tThese findings reframe recursive depth estimation from a classification problem to a measurement science problem, establishing that the actionable distinction is ternary: human-written vs.\\ first-generation vs.\\ deeply recursive text (73\\% accuracy).\n32\t\\end{abstract}\n33\t\n34\t% ============================================================\n35\t\\section{Introduction}\n36\t\n37\tThe proliferation of large language model (LLM) generated content on the web has created a new challenge for training data curation: \\emph{recursive contamination}.\n38\tWhen models are trained on data that includes their own outputs (or outputs of predecessor models), each generation cycle progressively degrades the statistical properties of the text \\citep{shumailov2024ai,drayson2025model}.\n39\tThis recursive feedback loop is not hypothetical---web crawls already contain substantial synthetic content, and each new generation of models trained on such crawls compounds the effect.\n40\t\n41\tExisting work addresses the \\emph{detection} problem: distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text \\citep{mitchell2023detectgpt,bao2024fastdetectgpt,tulchinskii2024diveye,deng2024editlens}.\n42\tHowever, detection is binary, while the recursive contamination problem is inherently \\emph{ordinal}: first-generation synthetic text may be nearly indistinguishable from human text and even beneficial for training, whereas deeply recursive text exhibits measurable quality degradation.\n43\tThe critical question is not ``is this synthetic?'' but ``how many recursive cycles has this undergone?''\n44\t\n45\tWe formalize this as the \\textbf{ordinal generation depth estimation} task and construct a controlled benchmark to study it systematically.\n46\tRather than proposing a method and demonstrating superiority, our primary contribution is \\emph{characterizing the fundamental limits} of depth estimation.\n47\tThrough the \\textbf{Depth Degradation Atlas}---a systematic mapping of how 19 statistical features evolve across depths, models, and decoding strategies---we establish the \\textbf{Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD)}, the depth beyond which adjacent generations become statistically indistinguishable.\n48\t\n49\tOur key findings are:\n50\t\\begin{enumerate}\n51\t    \\item \\textbf{EDD\\,$=$\\,2 is the dominant regime}: In 7 of 9 model--strategy combinations, reliable discrimination saturates at depth~2. Beyond this, recursive generation enters a statistical attractor where text properties fluctuate without systematic directional change.\n52\t    \\item \\textbf{Depth signals are model-specific}: Cross-model transfer accuracy is 33\\% (vs.\\ 42\\% within-model), and feature importance rankings differ substantially across models (Kendall's $\\tau = 0.52$). Practical depth estimation requires knowing which model generated the text.\n53\t    \\item \\textbf{The actionable granularity is ternary}: The distinction human / first-generation / deeply recursive achieves 73\\% accuracy and captures nearly all practical value, with downstream curation performance within 0.8~pp of fine-grained 6-class estimation.\n54\t\\end{enumerate}\n55\t\n56\tThese results reframe recursive depth estimation from a classification engineering problem to a measurement science problem.\n57\tWe release the benchmark, Atlas visualizations, and all evaluation code to support future work on this new task.\\footnote{Code and data: \\texttt{[anonymized for review]}}\n58\t\n59\t% ============================================================\n60\t\\section{Related Work}\n61\t\n62\t\\paragraph{AI-Generated Text Detection.}\n63\tBinary detection methods include statistical approaches based on log-probability curvature \\citep{mitchell2023detectgpt}, fast detection via conditional probability divergence \\citep{bao2024fastdetectgpt}, feature-based classifiers using diversity metrics \\citep{tulchinskii2024diveye}, and calibrated detection via binocular scoring \\citep{hans2024binoculars}.\n64\tEditLens \\citep{deng2024editlens} extends beyond binary classification to estimate the extent of AI editing, but targets single-pass edits rather than recursive depth.\n65\tAll of these approaches answer ``is this AI-generated?'' rather than ``how many recursive cycles has this undergone?''\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{Model Collapse and Recursive Degradation.}\n68\t\\citet{shumailov2024ai} demonstrated that iterative training on model outputs leads to irreversible distribution collapse, with later generations losing tail information.\n69\t\\citet{drayson2025model} provided a systematic empirical characterization of model collapse patterns across architectures and identified non-monotonic degradation in certain configurations.\n70\tThese studies assume depth is known \\emph{a priori} and characterize what happens at each depth; we ask whether depth can be \\emph{inferred} from text alone.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Ordinal Classification.}\n73\tCORAL \\citep{cao2020rank} introduced consistent rank logits for ordinal regression.\n74\tCLOC \\citep{dai2025cloc} extended contrastive learning to ordinal settings with learnable margins.\n75\tWe adapt CLOC to text depth estimation (DCOL) but find that in our setting, the learning objective contributes minimally compared to feature availability.\n76\t\n77\t% ============================================================\n78\t\\section{Task Formalization and Benchmark}\n79\t\n80\t\\subsection{Ordinal Depth Estimation}\n81\t\n82\tGiven a text passage $x$, we define its \\emph{generation depth} $d(x) \\in \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, K\\}$ where $d=0$ denotes human-written text and $d=k$ denotes text produced by the $k$-th recursive generation cycle.\n83\tThe task is to predict $d(x)$ from $x$ alone, respecting the ordinal structure: confusing depth~1 with depth~3 is worse than confusing depth~1 with depth~2.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Benchmark Construction}\n86\t\n87\tWe construct recursive generation chains using three model families and three decoding strategies:\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Models.} Pythia-1.4B (trained on The Pile), OLMo-1B (trained on Dolma), GPT-2~XL 1.5B (trained on WebText). These span different training corpora, architectures, and tokenizers.\n90\t\n91\t\\paragraph{Decoding Strategies.} Nucleus sampling ($p=0.95$), top-$k$ sampling ($k=50$), and temperature sampling ($T=0.8$). Each strategy produces different distributional characteristics in generated text.\n92\t\n93\t\\paragraph{Chain Construction.} Starting from human-written seed texts from each model's training domain, we generate depth-1 text by prompting the model with the seed, then generate depth-2 by prompting with depth-1 output, continuing to depth~5. This yields $3 \\times 3 \\times 6 = 54$ conditions.\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Features.} We extract 19 statistical features from each text passage: perplexity statistics (mean, variance, p10, p50, p90), surprisal statistics (mean, variance, p10, p50, p90), lexical diversity metrics (type-token ratio, Yule's K, vocab richness, hapax ratio), distributional shape features (zipf slope, burstiness), and structural features (mean sentence length, sentence length variance, repetition rate).\n96\t\n97\t% ============================================================\n98\t\\section{Depth Degradation Atlas}\n99\t\\label{sec:atlas}\n100\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fr9E31omErFZDmot9fqjaq","session_id":"bc99c231-f3c4-48af-92ec-23a6c87c30e4","uuid":"e071a4f5-4623-4477-bbd8-06f8362b2c68","timestamp":"2026-05-14T15:01:02.988Z"}
